  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Today into tonight, a shortwave trough could \nspark another round of showers and storms, but it will be \nworking against a relatively stable boundary layer.\n\nA Severe Thunderstorm Watch has been issued for the Eastern\nShore of Maryland today until 5 PM.\n\nA cold front continues to remain situated across central \nportions of the region. This has left a large portion of our \nregion in the relatively cooler marine layer and more stable air\nmass north of the front. The net result is that our region \nshould be in northeast to easterly flow, and cloudy conditions \nfor much of the day. As a result, surface based instability is \nexpected to be very limited, with mostly elevated instability \npresent. \n\nThe risk for severe storms still remains quite conditional and \nuncertain for a large portion of our area. There is currently a\nline of thunderstorms sinking to the southeast. The areas that\nhave the current best potential to see this convection is into\nDelmarva, particularly the Eastern Shore of Maryland which is\nunder the Severe Thunderstorm Watch through 5 PM. Given how \nsharp of a mid level short wave trough that is approaching, \nthere will be ample forcing, but it is a question of if the \nelevated instability will be enough to sustain storms through \nparts our region.\n\nWith that said, some CAMs do feature additional scattered \nstorms moving into parts of the area by late this\nafternoon/early evening and into tonight. Confidence in details\nregarding this remains relatively low, but should the region \nexperience more destabilization and erosion of the marine layer \nthan is currently anticipated, storms will be capable of \ndamaging winds, some hail, and perhaps an isolated tornado. The \ngreater risk of any severe storms will be toward our southern \nand western zones. Locally heavy rainfall will also be a threat \nwith any training showers and storms, even if a severe \nthunderstorm threat does not materialize. Portions of eastern PA\nhave been highlighted in a Slight Risk for excessive rainfall, \nwhich could lead to localized flash flooding.\n\nSPC maintains a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for severe\nthunderstorms for much of our area, but again, this risk is \nhighly conditional and low confidence for today into tonight.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions are forecast across \nthe region on Thursday. \n\nA strong marine layer influence today will keep highs mainly in\nthe 70s to low 80s in most areas. Following the brief cool down\nin the marine layer today for most areas, hot and humid \nconditions are once again expected across the region on Thursday\nas the frontal boundary lifts north again. With the front to \nthe north, high temperatures are forecast to climb into the \nupper 80s to mid 90s for many areas (low to mid 80s far north).\nThese temperatures combined with dewpoints in the low to mid \n70s will again result in heat indices well into the 90s to low \n100s south of I-78, perhaps even as high as 105 degrees for \nDelmarva.\n\nA new Heat Advisory was issued for portions of southeastern PA\nand NJ. This advisory may need to be expanded into Delmarva if\nthe forecast for heat indices near 105 degrees holds. North of\nthe current advisory area, maximum heat indices are forecast to\ntop out in the low to mid 90s, thus the advisory is unlikely to\nneed to be expanded farther north.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled conditions will continue on Thursday \nwith the potential for severe thunderstorms. Scattered \nthunderstorms are again possible on Friday, mainly south of \nPhiladelphia.\n\nAnother round of severe thunderstorms are possible on Thursday.\nFriday could also experience a few severe storms across \nDelmarva, depending on how quickly the frontal boundary pushes \nsouth.\n\nSPC has a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for severe \nthunderstorms on Thursday for Delmarva and a Marginal Risk \n(level 1) to as far north as the I-78 corridor. \n\nThe northwest flow pattern will remain in place on Thursday and\nwill continue to be supportive of all severe threats, including\ndamaging winds, large hail, and a few tornadoes, depending on \nhow all the mesoscale details pan out. The threat for flash \nflooding is typically lower with these patterns, but not zero. \nTraining can still occur and a single locale could have multiple\ndays with heavy rainfall.\n\nFriday's SPC outlook only includes general thunderstorms (no\nsevere risk), though there is still some uncertainty with this.\nOur latest forecast has about a 30-40% chance of showers and \nthunderstorms near and south of Philadelphia.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...The Labor Day Weekend will feature mostly dry \nweather with at or below normal temperatures and lower humidity.\n\nBy Saturday, current model consensus indicates the frontal \nboundary will be pushing well south of our region, with \nCanadian high pressure building across the Northeast and Mid-\nAtlantic. This will largely end any shower and thunderstorm \npotential. Thus, Labor Day Weekend is still shaping up to \nfeature nice weather, with tempe",
